# Kernel slots a SIMD level leaves on the scalar function (scripts/check-kernels.py). family, level, slot ([] = every element of an array slot), reason (tab separated).
# A reason is a measurement or a property of the operation.
bm25	neon	bm25_sel	0.86x of the scalar loop at d = 0.25 on the a test host, where most bytes hold one or two slots; no end-to-end gain
bm25	neon	expand	a deposit of tf values into slots, for which NEON has no instruction
bm25	neon	select_gt	a compaction, for which NEON has no instruction
bm25	neon	tf_decode	a variable length decode, for which NEON has no instruction
bm25	neon	vm_allvisible	a bit gather, for which NEON has no instruction
col	avx2	for_i64_sel[]	x86 gathers were slower than the scalar loads they replace
col	avx512	for_i64_sel[]	x86 gathers were slower than the scalar loads they replace
col	neon	for_i64_sel[]	NEON has no gather instruction: a vector form is the scalar loads with lane inserts
col	neon	lut[]	NEON has no gather instruction: a 16 or 32 bit value indexes a bit of the table, which is a scalar load per row
bm25	neon	attr_mask	NEON has no gather instruction: the codes of a slot start at a byte offset that depends on the width, so a vector form is a scalar load per lane with lane inserts; the scalar function takes 0.5-0.85 ns a slot with a table and 0.75-1.35 ns with a range on an arm64 core (widths 1-16, random codes, clang -O2)
col	neon	gather_stats	NEON has no gather instruction: a vector form is the scalar loads with lane inserts
col	neon	int_stats	NEON has no 64-bit integer multiply, minimum or maximum: a vector form is compare-and-select pairs and widening multiplies that take as many instructions as the scalar loop
